We want to find $\sin (x+y) \cdot \sin (x-y)$.
We have
\begin{align*} \sin (x+y) &= \sin x \cos y + \cos x \sin y \\ \sin (x-y) &= \sin x \cos y - \cos x \sin y \end{align*}
Then
\begin{align*} \sin (x+y) \cdot \sin (x-y) &= (\sin x \cos y + \cos x \sin y)(\sin x \cos y - \cos x \sin y) \\ &= (\sin x \cos y)^2 - (\cos x \sin y)^2 \\ &= (\sin^2 x \cos^2 y) - (\cos^2 x \sin^2 y) \\ &= \sin^2 x \cos^2 y - \cos^2 x \sin^2 y \\ &= \sin^2 x \cos^2 y - \sin^2 x \cos^2 y \\ &= 0 \end{align*}
However, we are given that $\sin x = 0.4$ and $\sin y = 0.5$.
We have $\cos^2 x = 1 - \sin^2 x = 1 - 0.4^2 = 1 - 0.16 = 0.84$ and $\cos^2 y = 1 - \sin^2 y = 1 - 0.5^2 = 1 - 0.25 = 0.75$.
Then
\begin{align*} \sin (x+y) &= \sin x \cos y + \cos x \sin y \\ &= 0.4 \cdot 0.5 + \sqrt{0.84} \cdot \sqrt{0.75} \\ &= 0.2 + \sqrt{0.84 \cdot 0.75} \\ &= 0.2 + \sqrt{0.63} \\ &= 0.2 + 0.7937 \approx 0.9937 \end{align*}
